From 9f88a551057eb706163d958db5fc8e7b1a072fce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:53:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 082/112] LPF: the 12 / 24 dB and drive ladders keep their
 state to 32 bits below the LSB below 4.5 kHz: no rounding noise, no dead band

Each ladder stage rounded its step to 2 LSB, so the state moved by 4 LSB at the least: at low cutoff (moveability down
to 0.002) differences under ~500 LSB moved nothing, and each sample's rounding added up in the state, amplified by the
pole. The output carried a fixed floor of rounding noise (-161 to -178 dBFS A-weighted against a float64 model at 28-63
Hz cutoff) and after the music the state stayed stuck (-114 to -175 dBFS instead of silence).

Below moveability 0.25 (about 4.5 kHz) the stages now add the exact product to a 64-bit state (LadderStage::
doFilterPrecise(), two smlal) whose high word is the state rounded to the LSB, so the feedback taps and the next stage
read it as before; per block, above it everything stays bit-exact. The analog noise on the cutoff is the same, worked
out from twice the moveability (one more bit). The error is now at the output word's own rounding: -183 to -200 dBFS
A-weighted (22 dB lower in every case), and the ladder falls silent after the music. Same response and self-oscillation
(output levels and tails equal to 0.1 dB). Cost on the Cortex-A9: +4 to +9 instructions per sample and channel
(LP24 res 30 mono 7418 -> 8601 per 128 samples), +27 KB code.

